# November 24, 2026: Utah, a third statewide scatter, from the Bryce Canyon Lodge and Kanab in the south to Randolph in the
# north-east corner and Wendover on the Nevada line. Utah already had 53 places; this batch adds eleven.
# Facts come from the linked sources, opened in this session: Wikipedia (read as raw text), the National Register forms read
# as text from NPGallery (Buckhorn Flat lime kiln 1980, Elsinore Sugar Factory 1980, Randolph Tabernacle 1986, Goulding's
# Trading Post 1980), the National Park Service pages for Hole-in-the-Rock and Bryce Canyon Lodge, the Utah State Parks pages
# for Camp Floyd and Utahraptor State Park, Parry Lodge's own site, Goulding's own site and the church's temple page.
from data_b import N
from data_ma import W
def L(t,u): return [t,u]
BATCH={'key':'ut_mzkf','label':'Utah: a third scatter, from Bryce Canyon to Randolph and Wendover','added':'2026-11-24 20:31'}
PLACES=[
N('camp-floyd-state-park-ut','Camp Floyd State Park Museum (Camp Floyd and the Stagecoach Inn)','Fairfield','ut','fort','standing',40.26048,-112.09263,'site','open','drive','forts','1858–1861',
  'In 1858 an army of more than 3,500 soldiers and civilians, sent by President Buchanan to deal with a feared Mormon rebellion, built Camp Floyd in Cedar Valley: 400 buildings by November 1858, mostly adobe, with enough civilians following to make Fairfield briefly almost half the size of Salt Lake City. No rebellion came. The post was renamed Fort Crittenden when its namesake, the Secretary of War, resigned to join the Confederacy, and was abandoned in July 1861, its buildings sold or torn down. The Stagecoach Inn nearby (the John Carson House) was a stage stop until the railroad of 1869 and a Pony Express stop in 1860 to 1861.',
  'What is left of the camp is the post cemetery and one commissary building, which was a roofless ruin in 1948 and was restored in 1960; the Stagecoach Inn is the park\'s headquarters and a museum, and the 1898 Fairfield District School stands at 59 North Church Street. Utah State Parks calls it three structures and a cemetery. Interior and exhibits not checked beyond the park\'s own description of a museum and the inn.',
  'A whole army camp reduced to one commissary, a cemetery and an inn.',
  'Run by Utah State Parks on state land. The park\'s page gives hours Monday to Saturday 9 a.m. to 5 p.m., closed Thanksgiving, Christmas and New Year\'s Day, telephone (801) 768-8932, and lists a gift shop, restrooms and a visitor center; the entrance fee is on a separate page not read. The same page prints the wrong town in its address block (Wallsburg), so use the pin; the three buildings are all within a short walk of each other in Fairfield. The pin is the geocoder\'s point for the Stagecoach Inn\'s street address, 18035 West Main Street, where the park has its headquarters; Wikipedia\'s and the Register\'s coordinate for the camp lies about 700 metres west in open sage and shows no building.',
  [W('Camp Floyd State Park Museum'),L('Utah State Parks: Camp Floyd State Park Museum','https://stateparks.utah.gov/parks/camp-floyd/')],
  conf='mixed',th=[40.26062,-112.09336],thn='Stagecoach Inn State Park lot, Main Street',brick='?',hours='Monday to Saturday 9 a.m. to 5 p.m.; closed Thanksgiving, Christmas and New Year\'s Day'),
N('hole-in-the-rock-ut','Hole-in-the-Rock','Escalante','ut','landmark','traces',37.25861,-110.9,'site','public','hike','','1879–1880',
  'The notch in the canyon rim above the Colorado River that a party of 250 Latter-day Saint men, women and children, with 83 wagons and over 1,000 head of livestock, blasted and picked wider through the winter of 1879 to 1880 so that wagons could be let down it. The Park Service puts the drop at nearly 2,000 feet at an average grade of 25 degrees, in places 45; the party got down on January 26, 1880, crossed on a ferry, and founded Bluff. A trip meant to take six weeks took six months. The Park Service says no lives were lost and two babies were born on the way. Other sources give slightly different counts of wagons and people.',
  'A narrow rocky passage that hikers can walk, half a mile each way (the Park Service says to allow at least an hour for the round trip), choked with boulders and debris that make the original road hard to pick out. Look for the miners\' stairs added by the Hoskaninni Mining Company about 1900 and for grooves left by wagon wheels and hubs. An information exhibit stands on the slope on the right as you come up from the lake.',
  'The crack in a cliff that wagons were let down in the winter of 1879.',
  'Glen Canyon National Recreation Area, National Park Service. By land it is the end of the Hole-in-the-Rock Road south-east of Escalante; the Park Service says parts are open to two-wheel drive with good clearance, that four-wheel drive is recommended and needed after heavy rain, to check at the Escalante Interagency Visitor Center first, to stay on designated roads and that off-road driving, mountain bikes included, is not allowed. By water it is at buoy 66 on Lake Powell. Carry water and wear sturdy shoes. Hours of the visitor center not checked. The pin is the Register\'s coordinate for the notch itself; the nearest mapped parking is the end of the Original Hole-in-the-Rock Road, about 240 metres south.',
  [W('Hole in the Rock (rock formation)'),W('Hole in the Rock Trail'),L('National Park Service: Hole-in-the-Rock (Glen Canyon)','https://www.nps.gov/glca/historyculture/holeintherock.htm')],
  conf='mixed',th=[37.25661,-110.90097],thn='Hole-in-the-Rock Road parking',brick='no'),
N('dalton-wells-moab-isolation-center','Dalton Wells CCC camp and Moab Isolation Center site','Moab','ut','inst','traces',38.71278,-109.69944,'site','public','drive','','1935–1943',
  'Built in 1935 as Camp DG-32, one of four Civilian Conservation Corps camps in Grand County, for up to 200 young men who worked on wells, trails and grazing land for the Division of Grazing. In 1942 to 1943 it was turned into the Moab Relocation Center, a place of isolation for Japanese Americans from other camps who were deemed troublemakers after unrest such as the Manzanar riot; Wikipedia says it never held many people and that some consider it illegal because Executive Order 9066 did not authorize it. A marker source says two inmates were killed by soldiers.',
  'Little survives of the camp. Sources describe a CCC-built concrete and rock cistern to the north, two explosives sheds dug into the hillside west across the highway, and concrete slabs, rock-outlined walkways and the cottonwood trees the enrollees planted. The site is on the National Register, listed 1994.',
  'A New Deal camp that was turned into a prison camp.',
  'Utah created Utahraptor State Park in 2021 to interpret this historic site, about 14 to 15 miles north-west of Moab off U.S. 191. The park lists a visitor center with hours of daily 9 a.m. to 5 p.m. March to November and Tuesday to Sunday 9 a.m. to 5 p.m. December to February, telephone (435) 259-3770; I did not find a page that says where on the grounds the camp remains are signed or whether they can be walked. A mapped lot on the Moab Isolation Center Trail lies 130 metres south-west of the pin. The pin is the Register coordinate, on bare ground with slabs, and the visitor center was not located.',
  [W('Dalton Wells Isolation Center'),L('Utah State Parks: Utahraptor State Park','https://stateparks.utah.gov/parks/utahraptor/')],
  conf='mixed',th=[38.7118,-109.70029],thn='Moab Isolation Center Trail lot',brick='?',hours='Park hours: daily 9 a.m. to 5 p.m. March to November; Tuesday to Sunday December to February'),
N('bryce-canyon-lodge-ut','Bryce Canyon Lodge and Deluxe Cabins','Bryce Canyon National Park','ut','hotel','standing',37.62670,-112.16786,'exact','open','drive','hotels','1924–1927',
  'A National Historic Landmark lodge built in 1924 and 1925 for the Utah Parks Company, the Union Pacific\'s tourist subsidiary, to the design of Gilbert Stanley Underwood. The Park Service calls it the only original lodge Underwood designed on the railroad\'s Grand Circle tour of the Grand Canyon\'s North Rim, Zion, Bryce and Cedar Breaks. Wikipedia gives the central block to 1924 and the wings to 1926; the Park Service says the final configuration was reached by 1927.',
  'A two-storey building on a stone base with oversized log framing, a long log porch and a steep shingled roof whose courses are laid in wave-like curves that make it look warped (a pattern repeated on every re-roofing). Inside, the lobby has milled timbers and a pointed-arch fireplace of rough stone, and the dining room and small auditorium are open to exposed log trusses. The deluxe cabins to the south-east have rubble stone chimneys and log-slab siding.',
  '',
  'In Bryce Canyon National Park (park entrance fee), between Sunset and Sunrise Points, managed by the concessioner Aramark. The Park Service says it has 114 rooms in the lodge, motel and cabins and a dining room serving breakfast, lunch and dinner, with a gift shop and parking, and to book ahead. The Park Service page gives no season or opening times, so those were not checked.',
  [W('Bryce Canyon Lodge'),L('National Park Service: Bryce Canyon Lodge','https://www.nps.gov/places/000/bryce-canyon-lodge.htm')],
  conf='',brick='no',hours='?'),
N('wendover-airfield-ut','Historic Wendover Airfield (Enola Gay hangar and the old air base)','Wendover','ut','fort','standing',40.72792,-114.02964,'exact','open','drive','forts','1940–1969',
  'Wendover Army Air Field was begun in September 1940 and grew to 668 buildings; it trained B-17 and B-24 crews, and in 1944 Colonel Paul Tibbets chose it to train the 509th Composite Group, the B-29 unit that dropped the atomic bombs, including testing of Little Boy and Fat Man shapes. The Air Force closed it in 1969 and gave it to Wendover City in 1977; Tooele County owns the airport and the buildings now. It was listed on the National Register in 1975 and the Enola Gay hangar was named one of America\'s Most Endangered Places in 2009, after which it was restored.',
  'A volunteer foundation has restored the control tower, a barracks, the service club and the Norden bombsight storage building and is still working on the administration building, dining hall, nurses\' quarters and B-29 hangar; one barracks burned in 2020. The museum shows a replica of the Little Boy bomb, a paper crane folded by Sadako Sasaki, uniforms and homefront items, with a C-54 Skymaster, a C-123 and an F-86L among the aircraft. Interior of the hangar not checked.',
  'Where the bomb crews trained, hangars and tower still standing.',
  'A county-owned working airport with the foundation\'s museum in the former operations building. Its own site refused the page reader and its archived copy could not be opened, so hours are not confirmed from the operator. A search result quoting a listing said spring and summer Monday to Friday 10 a.m. to 5 p.m. and Saturday 9 to 5, shorter weekday hours in fall and winter, closed Sunday, and other listings differ; telephone (435) 665-7724. Another listing gives admission as about $8 for adults in 2025. The federal land map shows the ground as part of the Utah Test and Training Range, but the sources above say the airport and buildings belong to Tooele County; go to the museum and stay out of fenced airfield areas. The pin is OpenStreetMap\'s point for the Enola Gay hangar, the big hangar by the apron; the museum is in the former operations building about 700 metres west (marked as the parking point).',
  [W('Historic Wendover Airfield'),W('Wendover Air Force Base')],
  conf='mixed',th=[40.72776,-114.03775],thn='Museum, former operations building',brick='?',hours='?'),
N('parry-lodge-kanab','Parry Lodge','Kanab','ut','hotel','standing',37.04839,-112.52688,'exact','open','drive','hotels','1892; motor court 1931',
  'In 1892 Justin "Jet" Johnson built a one-and-a-half storey frame farmhouse here; in 1928 three Parry brothers from Salt Lake City bought it and turned it into a motor court of two-room cabins, which opened for its first tourist season in June 1931. Film crews making Westerns stayed here from the late 1930s, and Kanab became known as "Little Hollywood". The lodge says John Wayne helped pay for the pool in the 1950s. It has been on the National Register since 2003.',
  'The Victorian Gothic Revival farmhouse of 1892 is still the heart of the lodge, its parlour now the lobby, with the later cabins and courtyard around it. It contains photographs of the actors who stayed, and Wikipedia says most rooms are named after them. Still a working hotel.',
  'A farmhouse turned motor court where the Western stars slept.',
  'A private working hotel at 89 East Center Street, Kanab, telephone (435) 644-2601; a guest can see the lobby and grounds, and anyone else can look from the street. Opening hours as such are not given; the site is for bookings.',
  [W('Parry Lodge'),L('Parry Lodge','https://www.parrylodge.com/')],
  conf='',brick='?',hours='Hotel with reception; book by telephone or on its site'),
N('gouldings-trading-post-ut','Goulding\'s Trading Post','Monument Valley','ut','landmark','standing',37.00693,-110.20353,'exact','open','drive','','1923–1924',
  'Harry Goulding and his wife Leone, "Mike", founded this trading post in 1923 and 1924 on the Utah side of the Arizona line, and ran it for forty years before giving it to Knox College in Illinois to fund Navajo scholarships (the 1980 Register form). When the director John Ford was looking for a site for Stagecoach in 1938 to 1939, Goulding took photographs of Monument Valley to send him, and Ford filmed there for years. The trading post became a lodge and museum. In November 2023 the Navajo Nation bought Goulding\'s Lodge for $59.5 million, according to Wikipedia.',
  'The 1924 main building, two storeys of locally quarried coursed sandstone with a porch across the front, and behind it a smaller building of 1923 with a log-and-sod gabled roof, used for storage in 1980; the motel and dining room beside are 1960s additions and not part of the Register listing. Wikipedia says the old building is now a museum.',
  '',
  'Goulding\'s Resort is a working lodge, restaurant and campground at the foot of Monument Valley, now owned by the Navajo Nation. Its own site lists rooms, the Stagecoach Restaurant, an RV park and tours, but gives no museum hours on the page read, so the museum\'s opening times were not checked. Telephone 866-313-9769. The pin is OpenStreetMap\'s point for the museum building; the Register\'s coordinate, a little to the north-east, falls on the shaded ground by the cliff. The land is inside the Navajo Nation Reservation as mapped.',
  [W('Goulding\'s Trading Post'),L('Goulding\'s Resort','https://www.gouldings.com/')],
  conf='mixed',brick='no',hours='?'),
N('manti-temple-ut','Manti Temple','Manti','ut','worship','standing',39.27291,-111.63376,'exact','view','drive','','1877–1888',
  'The Latter-day Saints\' fifth temple, dedicated in 1888 and the third built west of the Mississippi, on a limestone hill that Manti\'s settlers had quarried since 1849. Brigham Young announced it in 1875; ground was broken in April 1877. It stands on a partly levelled hill terraced with 2,400 cords of rock. Minerva Teichert\'s mural of 1947 in the World Room is among the art inside, and plans for the 2020s renovation led to petitions over the murals\' future.',
  'A castle-like building of locally quarried oolitic limestone with two towers, 179 feet on the east and 169 feet on the west, and two spiral staircases of 151 steps inside. The temple was rededicated in 2024 after a public open house in March and April of that year. Inside it is open only to church members with a recommend, so the pin gives a view of the outside from the grounds.',
  'A limestone temple on a hill, visible across the Sanpete Valley.',
  'Owned by the Church of Jesus Christ of Latter-day Saints; the church\'s page gives the address as 200 East 510 North, says there is no visitors\' center, and lists the temple\'s closures for 2026 and 2027. Whether the grounds are open to the public was not checked. The pin is the Wikipedia coordinate, on the building.',
  [W('Manti Utah Temple'),L('Church of Jesus Christ of Latter-day Saints: Manti Utah Temple','https://www.churchofjesuschrist.org/temples/details/manti-utah-temple')],
  conf='',brick='no'),
N('randolph-tabernacle-ut','Randolph Tabernacle (Randolph meetinghouse)','Randolph','ut','worship','standing',41.66513,-111.18569,'exact','view','drive','','1898–1914',
  'A large Victorian meetinghouse built by the Randolph Ward of the Latter-day Saints in Rich County between 1898 and 1914: planned in July 1898 as a hall big enough for people from outlying settlements, brick walls up by 1901, roof by 1902, first meeting in November 1904, tower finished in 1909 and dedication in 1914. The ward paid nearly all of the roughly $24,000 cost. Its architect John C. Gray was also the ward\'s bishop. Wikipedia notes it is technically a meetinghouse, not a tabernacle. Listed on the National Register in 1986.',
  'A one-storey brick building in Victorian Eclectic style, about 50 by 120 feet, with a two-storey tower, round-arched openings between false buttresses, fish-scale shingles and stickwork in the gables, on a rock and sandstone foundation. The 1986 form says the original features are well preserved apart from a large rear addition of the early 1980s. Interior not checked.',
  '',
  'Owned by the Church of Jesus Christ of Latter-day Saints and still used by the Randolph Ward, off Utah Route 16; look from the street. Times of services were not checked.',
  [W('Randolph Tabernacle'),L('National Register form, 1986 (NPGallery)','https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NRHP/86000724_text')],
  conf='',brick='yes'),
N('buckhorn-flat-lime-kiln-ut','Buckhorn Flat Lime Kiln (Denver and Rio Grande lime kiln)','Cleveland','ut','industry','ruins',39.18785,-110.70257,'exact','public','hike','','1881–1882',
  'A round rubble-stone kiln built by the Denver and Rio Grande Western Railroad in 1881 to 1882 to make the lime for mortar and cement for bridges and culverts on its planned grade across Buckhorn Flat. The railroad dropped that route about 1882 for one by way of Woodside and the Price River, so the kiln was left as what the Register form calls an isolated reminder of an abandoned scheme.',
  'In 1980 the walls of uncoursed rubble stood about 12 feet high, against an original height of about 16 feet, with an arched doorway on the south-west side; the conical roof had fallen in. Current condition not checked.',
  'A railroad\'s lime kiln for a line that was never built here.',
  'The 1980 Register form gives the owner as the Bureau of Land Management (San Rafael Resource Area) and the access as unrestricted; that was not rechecked on a BLM page, which refused the page reader. It is in the San Rafael Swell country on Buckhorn Flat, reached by dirt road; road condition and parking not checked. The pin is the Register\'s coordinate, checked on the aerial.',
  [W('Denver and Rio Grande Lime Kiln'),L('National Register form, 1980 (NPGallery)','https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NRHP/80003901_text')],
  conf='mixed',brick='no'),
N('elsinore-sugar-factory-ut','Elsinore Sugar Factory (Weight Station Inn)','Elsinore','ut','industry','ruins',38.68200,-112.12392,'site','view','drive','','1910',
  'A beet sugar factory built in 1910 by E. H. Dyer & Sons for the Utah-Idaho Sugar Company, which the Register form calls the single most important agri-business in Sevier County history for the years 1911 to 1928. By 1980 only two buildings of the complex were standing, the factory office and rooming house and a large warehouse; the form says the foundations of the main factory, the boiler and machine shop, the beet sheds and pulp pits could still be seen.',
  'Two red-brick buildings with segmental arched openings: a big gabled warehouse whose bays are divided by piers, with brick corbelling at the roof line that gives the effect of a cornice, and a one-and-a-half storey office and rooming house with a brick ground floor, a shingled upper floor and paired shed dormers. Current condition not checked.',
  '',
  'In 1980 the form gave the owner as a private person and the property as occupied, a private residence; present ownership not checked. No public access is stated, so look from the road only. The pin is placed on the cluster of old buildings and yards seen on the aerial, about 85 metres from the Register\'s coordinate, which fell on a round yard feature; the brick buildings were not told apart from the other buildings there.',
  [W('Elsinore Sugar Factory'),L('National Register form, 1980 (NPGallery)','https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NRHP/80003959_text')],
  conf='mixed',brick='yes'),
]
